      en: "The Linzolo Catholic Mission, located south of Brazzaville, is the oldest Catholic institution in the Republic of the Congo and a pivotal landmark in Central Africa's missionary history. It was established on September 20, 1883, by Father Prosper Augouard, who later earned the moniker 'Apostle of the Congo.' The mission site features a historic late 19th-century church, built with characteristic red bricks and a simple architectural style that reflects the early design of missionary outposts. Beyond its religious role, Linzolo served as a vital center for education and agriculture, hosting the region's first formal schools and vocational training workshops. Its hilltop location offers panoramic views of the surrounding countryside, highlighting the strategic importance of the site during the colonial era. Linzolo played a fundamental role in the spread of Christianity and Western-style education throughout what is now Congo-Brazzaville. Today, the mission remains a peaceful place of prayer and a protected historical monument, drawing pilgrims and history enthusiasts alike. The surrounding gardens and buildings tell the story of the early missionaries' hardships and their interactions with the local communities.",
